In [[geography]], a '''sound''' is a smaller body of water usually connected to a sea or an ocean. A ''[[List of bodies of water called sound|sound]]'' may be an [[inlet]] that is deeper than a [[Bight (geography)|bight]] and wider than a [[fjord]]; or  a narrow sea channel or an ocean channel between two land masses, such as a [[strait]]; or also a [[lagoon]] between a [[barrier island]] and the mainland.<ref>{{cite web|title=sound-3|url=http://www.thefreedictionary.com/sound|publisher=TheFreeDictionary.com|access-date=3 March 2013}}</ref><ref name="oed">{{cite web|title=sound-4|url=http://oxforddictionaries.com/definition/english/sound--4|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120819015149/http://oxforddictionaries.com/definition/english/sound--4|url-status=dead|archive-date=19 August 2012|work=Oxford English Dictionary|access-date=3 March 2013}}</ref>

{{short description|Basic unit of time in music and music theory}}
{{Other uses|beat music|beat (acoustics)|drum beat|beat (disambiguation)}}
[[File:Metric levels.svg|thumb|350px|Metric levels: beat level shown in middle with division levels above and multiple levels below.]]

In [[music]] and [[music theory]], the '''beat''' is the basic unit of time, the [[pulse (music)|pulse]] (regularly repeating event), of the ''mensural level''<ref>Berry, Wallace (1976/1986). ''[[iarchive:structuralfuncti0000berr/page/349/mode/2up|Structural Functions in Music]]'', p. 349. {{ISBN|0-486-25384-8}}.</ref> (or ''beat level'').<ref>Winold, Allen (1975). "Rhythm in Twentieth-Century Music", ''[[iarchive:aspectsoftwentie0000unse/page/213/mode/2up|Aspects of Twentieth-Century Music]]'', p. 213. With, Gary (ed.). Englewood Cliffs, New Jersey: Prentice–Hall. {{ISBN|0-13-049346-5}}.</ref> The beat is often defined as the rhythm listeners would tap their toes to when listening to a piece of music, or the numbers a musician [[counting (music)|counts]] while performing, though in practice this may be technically incorrect (often the first multiple level).{{clarify|date=October 2024}} In popular use, ''beat'' can refer to a variety of related concepts, including [[pulse (music)|pulse]], [[tempo]], [[meter (music)|meter]], specific [[rhythm]]s, and [[groove (popular music)|groove]].

Rhythm in music is characterized by a repeating sequence of [[#On-beat and off-beat|stressed and unstressed beats]] (often called "strong" and "weak") and divided into [[bar (music)|bars]] organized by [[time signature]] and [[tempo]] indications.

Beats are related to and distinguished from pulse, rhythm (grouping), and meter:

{{Blockquote|Meter is the measurement of the number of pulses between more or less regularly recurring accents.<!--3--> Therefore, in order for meter to exist, some of the pulses in a series must be accented—marked for consciousness—relative to others.<!--4--> When pulses are thus counted within a metric context, they are referred to as ''beats''.|[[Leonard B. Meyer]] and [[Grosvenor Cooper|Cooper]] (1960)<ref>Cooper, Grosvenor and Meyer, Leonard B. Meyer (1960). ''The Rhythmic Structure of Music'', p.3-4. University of Chicago Press. {{ISBN|0-226-11521-6}}/{{ISBN|0-226-11522-4}}.</ref>}}

[[Meter (music)#Metric structure|Metric levels]] faster than the beat level are division levels, and slower levels are multiple levels. Beat has always been an important part of music. Some [[music genre]]s such as [[funk]] will in general de-emphasize the beat, while others such as [[disco]] emphasize the beat to accompany dance.<ref name="hhd">{{cite book |title=Hip Hop Dance |last=Rajakumar |first=Mohanalakshmi |year=2012 |publisher=ABC-CLIO |isbn=9780313378461 |page=5 |url=https://play.google.com/store/books/details?id=jGZhfy9UaGIC |access-date=22 November 2016}}</ref>
